  "fatal_path_source_review": {
    "assertion_path": "__assert_func -> esp_system_abort -> panic_abort: all IRAM. panic_abort stores the stack-buffer pointer and fatal flag in DRAM, then executes ill; it does not resume the ISR.",
    "exception_path": "IRAM panicHandler and xt_unhandled_exception call IRAM panic_enable_cache. When cache is disabled, it calls esp_ipc_isr_stall_abort then spi_flash_enable_cache -> spi_flash_restore_cache -> esp_cache_resume_ext_mem_cache -> cache_hal_resume. Linked restoration code/ctx are internal and Cache_Resume_ICache/Cache_Resume_DCache targets are ROM.",
    "first_post_restore_flash_use": "panic_enable_cache then calls flash-resident esp_cache_err_acs_save_and_clr. Its caller subsequently enters flash-resident panic_handler. Their ordering is after either the cache-enabled predicate branch or the cache-restoration call.",
    "config": "CONFIG_ESP_PANIC_HANDLER_IRAM is disabled and CONFIG_ESP_SYSTEM_PANIC_PRINT_REBOOT is enabled in both current builds. The full fatal handler intentionally depends on restored flash access.",
    "limit": "The original ISR closure walker stops at ill and does not automatically traverse CPU exception edges. This follow-up separately inspected the relevant entry/restoration sequence, not the complete exception-vector, diagnostic, peripheral-reset and reboot graph."
  },
